## `lightning/bolts` — BOLT#11's own worked example

  https://github.com/lightning/bolts
  Licensed under CC-BY 4.0: http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
  (stated in the repository's own README.md; there is no separate LICENSE
  file). The same upstream and the same terms the siblings
  `modules/lnwire/NOTICE` and `modules/lninvoice/NOTICE` already record; those
  two pin commit `35e79db504560b9d3494a0ed07bf1e8379c3663a`, and this module
  takes the same published example.

`src/ecdsa_recover.zig`'s `spec_privkey`, `spec_node_id`, `spec_hash`,
`spec_r`/`spec_s`/`spec_recid` are BOLT#11's first worked example ("Please make
a donation of any amount…"): the private key and node id printed literally in
the specification, the SHA-256 of its signing preimage, and the 65 signature
bytes carried by the `lnbc1pvjluez…` string the spec prints. They are the
repository's only offline secp256k1 RFC-6979 anchor — RFC 6979's own Appendix
A.2 covers DSA and the NIST curves only.

⛔ CORRECTED 2026-09-09: `ecdsa_recover.zig` described these as "a test oracle
under NOTICE policy §0". They are not. §0's oracle carve-out is for the
observable behaviour of a program that was RUN; these are values printed in a
specification document, which §0 separates out explicitly ("numbers read out of
an upstream source file by a script are reproduced data, not oracle output").
Reading them as oracle output also put this module on the opposite side of its
own siblings' answer about the identical upstream — `lnwire` and `lninvoice`
record `lightning/bolts` as CC-BY 4.0 and attribute it. It is attributed here
too, and the source comment has been corrected to match.

CC-BY 4.0 asks for attribution to the licensor, a link to the licence, and an
indication of whether the material was modified. The first two are above. As to
the third: NOT MODIFIED. Every value is the published one, hex-decoded from the
form the specification prints — a change of encoding, not of content. CC-BY 4.0
is not reproduced in full here because it does not ask to be: §3(a)(1)(C) is
satisfied by "a URI or hyperlink to this Public License".

Implementation vs. reproduced-value distinction: the field, group, scalar and
signature code in this module is its own clean-room work from the public
secp256k1 parameters and BIP340, which needs no attribution per root `NOTICE`
§0. What is reproduced, and only here, is published VECTOR AND EXAMPLE DATA.

## `C2SP/wycheproof` — ECDSA secp256k1/SHA-256 verification vectors (audit G3, added 2026-09-10)

  https://github.com/C2SP/wycheproof
  Licensed under the Apache License 2.0: http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0
  (stated in the repository's own README.md, "What license is Wycheproof
  offered under?"). Apache-2.0 §4 asks for three things MIT does not: that a
  copy of the License travel with the material (§4(a)), that modified files
  say they were changed (§4(b)), and that upstream copyright and attribution
  notices be retained (§4(c)). All three are discharged here, the same way
  the sibling `modules/p256/NOTICE` already discharges them for the P-256
  counterpart of one of these two files.

**Why this section exists.** Audit G3 (2026-09-04) found this module shipped
**zero** ECDSA test vectors of its own — only BIP340 (Schnorr) vectors, see
above — and that the gap was measurable: 7 of the module's own 15 targeted
mutations survived its test suite, and Wycheproof killed two of them with an
**accepted forgery** (`r >= n` silently reduced instead of rejected, tcId
116/132/245; `s >= n` silently reduced, tcId 133). Fixed 2026-09-10 by the
same mechanism the sibling `p256` module already uses:
`modules/k256/tools/gen-k256-wycheproof.py` (this repository's own MIT work) converts
two upstream Wycheproof JSON files into committed `.zig` fixtures.

**What exactly was taken**

  - `src/wycheproof_kat_vectors.zig` — 234 vectors (167 must verify, 67 must
    not), from `testvectors_v1/ecdsa_secp256k1_sha256_p1363_test.json` (234
    of 252 upstream rows; 18 dropped because their signature is not exactly
    64 bytes — `sign.ecdsaVerify`'s parameter is `[64]u8`, so a wrong-length
    signature cannot be handed to it at all).
  - `src/wycheproof_bitcoin_vectors.zig` — 123 vectors (109 must verify, 14
    must not), from `testvectors_v1/ecdsa_secp256k1_sha256_bitcoin_test.json`
    (123 of 463 upstream rows; 340 dropped because their DER encoding is not
    strictly canonical — this module has no DER parser, `sign
    .ecdsaVerifyLowS` takes raw `r || s`, so a non-canonical DER encoding is
    not this module's surface at all).

    Total: 357 vectors. Both fetched from
    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/C2SP/wycheproof/main/ on 2026-09-10.

It is not only numbers: the vectors carry 107 distinct authored English
description strings ("r is negative", "s is small and negative", "signature
malleability", "edge case for Shamir multiplication") reproduced verbatim as
each vector's `.comment` field —

    grep -ho '\.comment = "[^"]*"' modules/k256/src/wycheproof_*_vectors.zig \
      | LC_ALL=C sort -u | wc -l          ->  107

That is authorial prose, not a fact with one possible expression, so the
merger-doctrine reasoning the root `NOTICE` applies to specification-dictated
hex constants does not reach it.

**Verified, not assumed.** On 2026-09-10 both upstream JSON files were
fetched and every row present in the committed `.zig` files was compared
field by field against upstream, matched on upstream `tcId`: 234/234 P1363
rows and 123/123 Bitcoin rows identical on `msg`, `should_verify` and
`comment` (the P1363 file's `sig` too — the Bitcoin file's `sig` is a DER→raw
conversion, checked instead by the generator's own `der_to_raw` producing a
value `sign.ecdsaVerifyLowS` accepts/rejects exactly as upstream's verdict
says, i.e. by the test suite itself). 0 mismatches.

Upstream copyright notice, retained per Apache-2.0 §4(c)
----------------------------------------------------------

    Copyright 2016-2026 The Wycheproof Authors, offered under the
    Apache License 2.0.

(Upstream states this in its `README.md`. The repository's `LICENSE` is the
unfilled Apache-2.0 boilerplate, so its appendix `Copyright [yyyy] [name of
copyright owner]` line is not itself a notice; the README line above is.
Checked 2026-09-10 at https://github.com/C2SP/wycheproof — same finding the
`p256` NOTICE already recorded for the same upstream.)

Statement of changes, per Apache-2.0 §4(b)
---------------------------------------------

The upstream JSON was transformed into the two Zig files by
`modules/k256/tools/gen-k256-wycheproof.py` (MIT, this repository's own work — modeled
directly on the sibling `scripts/gen/gen-p256-wycheproof.py`). What it changes:

  1. **Re-encoded.** Each JSON test object becomes a Zig struct literal.
     Fields kept: the enclosing test group's `publicKey.uncompressed`, and
     the test's `tcId`, `msg`, `sig` (converted from DER for the Bitcoin
     file), `result` and `comment`.
  2. **Flattened.** Upstream test-group nesting is discarded; each group's
     public key is repeated onto every row that used it.
  3. **Fields dropped.** Not reproduced: the group-level `publicKeyDer`,
     `publicKeyPem`, `publicKeyJwk`, `type`, `source`, `sha`; the per-test
     `flags`; and the file-level `algorithm`, `schema`, `numberOfTests`,
     `header`, `notes`. The `notes` dictionary (authored prose describing
     each bug class) is not reproduced.
  4. **`result` collapsed to a boolean.** `should_verify = (result ==
     "valid")`. Measured: only `"valid"`/`"invalid"` occur in these two
     files, so upstream's third possible value (`"acceptable"`) never comes
     up here.
  5. **DER converted to raw `r || s`** (Bitcoin file only), keeping only
     rows whose DER is strictly canonical — see "what exactly was taken"
     above for the count.
  6. **18 / 340 rows omitted** (P1363 / Bitcoin) for the length/canonicality
     reasons above. Both generated files print the omission count into
     their own header, so it stays visible.

Both generated files carry a `// Generated by modules/k256/tools/gen-k256-wycheproof.py`
line naming the generator and their upstream JSON file in the module doc
comment — where §4(b)'s "prominent notice" lands for a file whose entire body
is the modification.

Apache-2.0 §4(d): nothing to propagate
------------------------------------------

§4(d) applies only if the Work includes a `NOTICE` text file as part of its
distribution. C2SP/wycheproof does not (checked 2026-09-10, same finding
`p256`'s NOTICE already recorded: no `NOTICE`/`NOTICE.txt` at the repository
root or via `raw.githubusercontent.com`). Nothing to propagate here.

Scope of this section
-------------------------

This section is about the reproduced Wycheproof vectors and nothing else.
The rest of this module — and the rest of this file — is unaffected; see the
sections above for the BIP340 and BOLT#11 attributions, which are separate
upstreams under separate terms.

The two generated files' `// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT` header refers to
the zig-libs-authored scaffolding they contain (the `Vector` struct, the doc
comments, the generator's output shape). The vector data and the 107
distinct `comment` strings are Wycheproof's, and they are Apache-2.0.


